lumns). So we have exactly n tiles covering all squares except the uncovered ones. So the construction works.\n\nThus the answer 2025 is correct.\n\nNow Level 2: Check reasoning validity. The solution uses valid methods: representation of uncovered squares as permutation matrix, matrix rank argument, Boolean rank, eigenvalue analysis, construction. All are valid mathematical methods. The solution also uses the fact that Boolean rank >= ordinary rank, which is a known inequality. The argument \"since each entry of the Boolean sum is either 0 or 1, and the ordinary sum yields the same 0/1 pattern (overlaps only increase entries beyond 1, but rank is unaffected by such increases)\" is a bit informal but the core idea is correct: if you have a Boolean decomposition M = OR_{k=1}^r A_k, then the real sum of the same matrices (with addition) yields a matrix that has entries >= M (i.e., entries are at least 1 where M has 1). However, the rank of M is <= rank of the sum, but the sum may have entries >1. But rank is defined for real matrices, and if you have entries >1, the rank may be higher? Actually rank of a matrix with entries >1 can be larger than rank of the binary matrix? Let's examine: Suppose M is binary, and we have A_k binary rank-1 matrices. The sum S = sum A_k yields a matrix with integer entries (counts of overlapping ones). The rank of S may be larger than rank of M? For example, M = [[1,0],[0,1]] (identity). It has rank 2. Suppose we decompose M as OR of rank-1 matrices: A1 = [[1,0],[0,0]] (rank1), A2 = [[0,0],[0,1]] (rank1). Then S = [[1,0],[0,1]] = M, rank2. So rank(S) = rank(M). In general, the sum of rank-1 matrices can have rank up to r, but rank can be less if there is linear dependence. The inequality rank(M) <= sum rank(A_k) = r holds. So the argument is valid.\n\nThus the reasoning is valid.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. We need to check for any justification gaps or critical errors.\n\nPotential issues: The solution uses Boolean rank concept