al \\([a,b]\\) and column interval \\([c,d]\\) (\\(a\\le b,\\;c\\le d\\)).  \nIf \\(T\\) contains a left‑adjacent square \\(L_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\), then \\(c\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\le d\\); consequently the whole column interval \\([c,d]\\) lies to the left of the hole in row \\(i\\), i.e. \\(d<\\sigma(i)\\).  \nFor any other row \\(k\\) in \\([a,b]\\) the rectangle must avoid the hole \\((k,\\sigma(k))\\); thus we must also have \\(d<\\sigma(k)\\). Hence the column interval of \\(T\\) is **strictly left** of all holes \\(\\sigma(k)\\) for rows \\(k\\in[a,b]\\).\n\nSimilarly, if \\(T\\) contains a right‑adjacent square \\(R_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\), then its column interval satisfies \\(c>\\sigma(i)\\), and consequently \\(c>\\sigma(k)\\) for every row \\(k\\in[a,b]\\); the column interval is **strictly right** of all those holes.\n\nTherefore a rectangle cannot simultaneously be strictly left of the holes of its rows and strictly right of them; it cannot contain a left‑adjacent square and a right‑adjacent square (even from different rows).  \nIf it contained two left‑adjacent squares \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) with \\(i<j\\), then the column interval would have to be left of both \\(\\sigma(i)\\) and \\(\\sigma(j)\\). Since \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq\\sigma(j)\\), the interval would have to lie left of the smaller of the two, and consequently would also contain the column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) (the hole of row \\(i\\)), contradicting the definition of a tile. An analogous contradiction occurs for two right‑adjacent squares. ∎\n\nBecause each of the \\(2n-2\\) squares in (1) must be covered and no tile can cover two of them, at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any admissible tiling uses }\\ge 2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal squares  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i