 least\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if }r_c=1\\text{ or }r_c=n,\\\\[2mm]\n2 & \\text{otherwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, exactly one column has its uncovered square in the top row\n(\\(r_{\\sigma(1)}=1\\)) and exactly one column has its uncovered square in the bottom row\n(\\(r_{\\sigma(n)}=n\\)).  All remaining \\(n-2\\) columns have the uncovered square in an interior row.\nSumming the above lower bounds over all columns gives\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;1+1+2\\,(n-2)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus any tiling satisfying the problem\u2019s condition uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) we place at most two horizontal tiles:\n\n* If \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) we place the tile\n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text{left}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n  \\]\n  which is a \\(1\\times(\\sigma(i)-1)\\) rectangle covering the whole left part \\(L_i\\).\n\n* If \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) we place the tile\n  \\[\n  T_i^{\\text{right}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n  a \\(1\\times\\bigl(n-\\sigma(i)\\bigr)\\) rectangle covering the whole right part \\(R_i\\).\n\nThe two tiles in the same row are separated by the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\), hence they are\ndisjoint.  Tiles belonging to different rows lie in different rows, so no two tiles intersect.\nAll cells of the board except the \\(n\\) uncovered squares are covered, because each cell belongs\neither to a left part \\(L_i\\) or a right part \\(R_i\\) of its row.\n\nExactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\); for that row the left tile is empty and is omitted.\nExactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\); for that row the right tile is empty and is omitted.\nConsequently the total number of tiles used is\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(n-1\\bigr)+\\bigl(n-1\\bigr)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles,\nwhile construction (2) exhibits a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
