:i<r_j\\},\\qquad   \n\\mathcal L:=\\{(i,j):i>r_j\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA rectangle is a product \\(I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are contiguous intervals of rows and columns, respectively.  \nIf a rectangle contains a column \\(j\\in J\\) and a row \\(i\\in I\\) with \\(i=r_j\\), then the uncovered square \\((r_j,j)\\) would belong to the rectangle, which is forbidden.  \nHence for every column \\(j\\in J\\) the whole interval \\(I\\) must lie either in \\([1,r_j-1]\\) or in \\([r_j+1,n]\\).  \nTherefore a rectangle can be placed only inside \\(\\mathcal U\\) or only inside \\(\\mathcal L\\); it cannot contain squares from both sets.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   Minimal number of rectangles for a histogram  \n\nConsider first \\(\\mathcal U\\).  \nFor each column \\(j\\) the height of \\(\\mathcal U\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nh_j:=r_j-1\\qquad (0\\le h_j\\le n-1).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal U\\) is a histogram with column heights \\((h_1,\\dots ,h_n)\\).  \nA partition of a histogram into axis‑parallel rectangles can be performed by scanning the columns from left to right.  \n\n*At column 1* we need \\(h_1\\) rectangles whose left side is at column 1.  \n*When moving from column \\(j-1\\) to column \\(j\\)*, if the height increases, i.e. \\(h_j>h_{j-1}\\), we must start \\(h_j-h_{j-1}\\) new rectangles at column \\(j\\); if the height decreases, some of the already existing rectangles simply end.  \n\nConsequently the number of rectangles that start (and therefore the total number of rectangles) is  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\mathcal U}=h_1+\\sum_{j=2}^{n}\\max\\{0,h_j-h_{j-1}\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(h_j=r_j-1\\) and \\(h_{j-1}=r_{j-1}-1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\mathcal U}= (r_1-1)+\\sum_{j=2}^{n}\\max\\{0,r_j-r_{j-1}\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe same argument applied to the lower region \\(\\mathcal L\\) (whose column heights are \\(h'_j=n-r_j\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\mathcal L}= (n-r_1)+\\sum_{j=2}^{n}\