yields  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\mathcal L}= (n-r_1)+\\sum_{j=2}^{n}\\max\\{0,r_{j-1}-r_j\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   A universal lower bound  \n\nAdding (9) and (10) gives for any tiling of the whole board  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nN_{\\text{total}}\n   &=N_{\\mathcal U}+N_{\\mathcal L}\\\\\n   &=\\bigl(r_1-1+n-r_1\\bigr)\n     +\\sum_{j=2}^{n}\\bigl(\\max\\{0,r_j-r_{j-1}\\}\n                         +\\max\\{0,r_{j-1}-r_j\\}\\bigr)\\\\\n   &= (n-1)+\\sum_{j=2}^{n}|r_j-r_{j-1}|.\n\\tag{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause the numbers \\(r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\) form a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), any two consecutive terms differ by at least \\(1\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n|r_j-r_{j-1}|\\ge 1\\qquad (j=2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nSummation of (12) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=2}^{n}|r_j-r_{j-1}|\\ge n-1.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nInsert (13) into (11) to obtain the universal lower bound  \n\n\\[\nN_{\\text{total}}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   A construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor every column \\(j\\) place at most two vertical rectangles:\n\n* if \\(r_j>1\\) place the rectangle \\(R^{\\uparrow}_j=[1,r_j-1]\\times\\{j\\}\\) (the cells above the hole);\n* if \\(r_j<n\\) place the rectangle \\(R^{\\downarrow}_j=[r_j+1,n]\\times\\{j\\}\\) (the cells below the hole).\n\nExactly one column has \\(r_j=1\\) (no rectangle above) and exactly one column has \\(r_j=n\\) (no rectangle below); all other columns contribute two non\u2011empty rectangles.  \nHence the total number of rectangles in this tiling is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nSince (15) meets the lower bound (14), the construction is optimal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   Determination of the minimum  \n\nFrom (14) and (15) we conclude that the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the smallest number of rectangular tiles that Matilda can place while leaving exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).**"
