onstruction with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles match, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently the set \\(U\\) of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there is a bijection\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to the diagonal case  \n\nIf we simultaneously permute the rows by a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) and the columns by the same\npermutation \\(\\sigma\\), the board is merely relabelled and the number of tiles required does not change.\nApplying the permutation \\(\\sigma\\) that sends \\(p(i)\\) to \\(i\\) for every \\(i\\) transforms the set \\(U\\) into the\nmain diagonal \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\).  \nHence it suffices to determine the minimum number of axis‑aligned rectangles that partition the\noff‑diagonal cells of an \\(n\\times n\\) grid.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nDefine, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_k^{\\text{low}} &=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\; 1\\le j\\le k\\},\\\\[2mm]\nR_k^{\\text{up}}  &=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\; k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach set \\(R_k^{\\text{low}}\\) (resp. \\(R_k^{\\text{up}}\\)) is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines.\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{R}= \\{R_k^{\\text{low}},R_k^{\\text{up}}\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any off‑diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).\n\n- If \\(i>j\\) (the cell lies strictly below the diagonal), set \\(k=j\\).  \n  Then \\(i\\ge k+1\\) and \\(j\\le k\\), so \\((i,j)\\in R_k^{\\text{low}}\\).\n\n- If \\(i<j\\) (the cell lies strictly above the diagonal), set \\(k=i\\).  \n  Then \\(i\\le k\\) and \\(j\\ge k+1\\), so \\((i,j)\\in R_k^{\\text{up}}\\).\n\nThus every off‑diagonal cell belongs to exactly one rectangle of \\(\\mathcal{R}\\).\n\n*Disjointness.*  \nIf two distinct rectangles of \\(\\mathcal{R}\\) intersect, they would have to share a row and a column,\nwhich would force a diagonal cell to belong to both – impossible because each rectangle is defined\nby a column interval that avoids the diagonal. A direct inspection shows that the row intervals of\ndifferent \\(R_k^{\\text{low}}\\) (resp. of different \\(R_k^{\\text{up}}\\)) are nested but never overlap\nwith the same column interval, and a lower‑left rectangle never meets an upper‑right rectangle.\nHence the rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n\nConsequently the off‑diagonal cells can be tiled with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.  \nFor \\(n=2025\\) this yields a tiling with \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nAssume again that the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells.  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{(i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll elements of \\(S\\) are off‑diagonal, so they must be covered by the rectangles.\n\n**Lemma.**  \nNo axis‑aligned rectangle that avoids all diagonal cells can contain two distinct elements of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(Q\\) contains \\((a,a+1)\\) and \\((c,c+1)\\) with \\(a<c\\).  \nThen the row interval of \\(Q\\) contains \\([a,c]\\) and the column interval contains \\([a+1,c+1]\\).\nBecause \\(a+1\\le c\\), the column interval also contains column \\(a+1\\); consequently\n\\(Q\\) contains the cell \\((a+1,a+1)\\), which lies on the diagonal – a contradiction.\nT